								<div class="title" id="post-title-4857203">
																		Re: SCP Concept: Guitar Hero
															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=5336470&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609337729" alt="cybersqyd" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=5336470)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/cybersqyd" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(5336470); return false;" >cybersqyd</a></span> <span class="odate time_1609319577 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">30 Dec 2020 09:12</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4857203">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">[+]&nbsp;Open&nbsp;for&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Not really a fan of this as a whole. It feels&#8230;too much like a generic murder monster withy a lot of additional complexity that just, isn't very interesting to me; which isn't helped by the central narrative section just expanding more on what the SCP does. What I really want to see out of a pitch like this is an indication you've considered how you can explore what the SCP does in an interesting way, or like have built up some kind of story about the SCP and the Foundation's interactions with it that really emphasises why it's interesting to read about.</p>
<hr />
<p><em>As an aside, murder monsters aren't inherently a bad idea to write about as a SCP; but they do present challenges to writing. Namely, you aren't trying to write a DnD Monster Manual style entry, and danger alone isn't interesting; so you have to figure out how you can make the danger feel interesting. Often, this is easiest by putting characters the reader cares about in danger (even in a very stripped down way e.g. <a href="/scp-3794">SCP-3794</a>, or providing clear stakes to the danger and having the threat be like, present. It's also possible to make it work if the danger is sufficiently interesting alone: something like <a href="/scp-5852">SCP-5852</a> is a great example of that; where the core is very simple but the threat is effective nevertheless.</em></p>
<hr />
<p>I think SCP-XXXX-ɑ is the best example of my issue with this: rather than relying on the user of the guitar being like 'wow I'm great at this I have to keep playing this', you've added a chemical it emits that basically forces people to keep playing it; and it feels like added complexity that doesn't actually make this more interesting. In general: it's a lot more interesting when people choose to do things instead of being forced to by a SCP because the agency that choosing grants allows you to really explore <em>why</em> someone would choose to do it and the conflict that choosing to continue doing something even once you begin to see the dangerous effects come in creates.</p>
<p>This isn't to say that's the only issue: the issue with this pitch at it's core is that instead of keeping the anomaly simple and doing something interesting with it, you've extended it's behaviour and the range of anomalous things it can do in an attempt at making it more interesting. My general advice for anomaly design is 'you should be able to describe what the anomaly does fully in one sentence': it doesn't apply always, but severely limiting the scope of the concept like that allows you to really focus on why the anomaly is interesting and how it's effects affect the world at large in an easier way.</p>
</div>
</div>
